Security coverage for source-built FFmpeg
========================================

The FFmpeg source build is not an Alpine FFmpeg package. OS/language package
scanners alone may miss it, and binary autodetection did not identify the
inspected shared build. An empty package scan is insufficient evidence.

scripts/check_native_media_security.py resolves a local image to an immutable
ID and creates an inspection container that is never started. It disables pulls
and networking and overrides declared image volumes with tmpfs to avoid creating
anonymous persistent volumes. It removes only its own labeled container. It does
not contact cameras, start media streams, mount the Docker socket into an image,
or execute the inspected image's programs. Docker CLI access remains privileged.

The checker reads bounded regular files from docker cp's tar stream without
extracting archive paths. It compares the source lock and recipes with the
checked-out repository, verifies the bundled upstream archive against its pin,
and verifies all four media executables and seven shared-library files against
the recorded build hashes. The source signature is verified during the image
build; this inspection checks the pinned material. A self-reported build ledger
is not signed container provenance or proof of reproducible compilation. Keep
image signing/attestation and actual runtime qualification as separate gates.

It creates an explicit CycloneDX FFmpeg component bound to the inspected image,
source archive and runtime inventory, then scans its CPE with Grype. Matching
must be enabled, the database valid and no more than 24 hours old, and findings
must not be suppressed. A synthetic FFmpeg 4.0 metadata-only control must match
known vulnerabilities; no old executable is installed or run. Any actual native
finding requires review and fails this gate. Current database results cannot
establish the absence of undisclosed or unrecorded vulnerabilities.

CI requires this check for backend and go2rtc alongside the complete-image
Trivy scan, preserving JSON evidence even on failure. CI pins the Grype download
by version and SHA-256. The local security-audit.py --docker/all path requires
the native check for BACKEND_IMAGE and, when selected, GO2RTC_IMAGE, using the
immutable image ID from Trivy's report. Missing tools, source material, scanner
results or identity binding fail rather than becoming a clean scan. Use Linux
and a new --work-dir for each attempt; the evidence directory is intentionally
not overwritten. These checks do not expand the supported architecture/hardware
matrix or establish an enterprise service-level guarantee.
